 P and dinner will be provided. We look forward to seeing you there!\n\nPap
 er Abstract: “Witch” and “witchcraft” have been among the most pot
 ent religious labels wielded by enslavers\, colonizers\, and purveyors of 
 hegemonic cultures to limit acceptable performances of religiosity along r
 acial and cultural lines. Even so\, ideas around religion and “superstit
 ion” are rarely cited as central racializing and gendering forces in the
  modern era beyond the role of Christianity in justifying slavery and colo
 nialism. Through the concept of “racialized diabolism\,” the chapter e
 xcerpt traces the discursive history of the concept of witchcraft and it (
 ab)uses in the development of racial hierarchies in the African Atlantic. 
 In doing so\, it demonstrates how religious concepts and imaginings like w
 itchcraft enfleshed the increasingly robust and iterative descriptions of 
 Guinean/African/Negro/Black culture as Western African\, Western European\
 , and Indigenous American worlds collided around the Atlantic.\n\nAlexis W
 ells-Oghoghomeh is an Associate Professor of Religious Studies and affilia
 te of the department of African and African American Studies and Program i
